From 44ad836abf080db9bdb9465f8454800d17cb7b57 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Marco Casaroli <marco.casaroli@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 17:38:16 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] fs/fat: Write the zeros of ftruncate() to the volume.

When ftruncate() makes a file longer, fat_dirextend() writes zeros to the
new part of the file in the sector cache of the handle.  The last of these
sectors stayed in that cache, and the volume kept the old data of the
sector.  Each handle has its own sector cache, so another handle of the
same file read the old data from the volume: bytes that were cut off by an
earlier ftruncate(), or the data of a deleted file on a new cluster.

fat_truncate() already writes back and drops the caches of all handles of
the file before it extends the file.  Write the last sector back at the
end of fat_dirextend(), so that the other handles read the zeros.

Assisted-by: Claude Code:claude-opus-5-5
Signed-off-by: Marco Casaroli <marco.casaroli@gmail.com>
---
 fs/fat/fs_fat32util.c | 10 ++++++++++
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+)

diff --git a/fs/fat/fs_fat32util.c b/fs/fat/fs_fat32util.c
index 5cfde8afeb46a..2ef84b5226146 100644
--- a/fs/fat/fs_fat32util.c
+++ b/fs/fat/fs_fat32util.c
@@ -1777,6 +1777,16 @@ int fat_dirextend(FAR struct fat_mountpt_s *fs, FAR struct fat_file_s *ff,
       sectndx    = pos & SEC_NDXMASK(fs);
     }
 
+  /* Write the last sector back.  The other handles of the file read it
+   * from the volume, and must see the zeros.
+   */
+
+  ret = fat_ffcacheflush(fs, ff);
+  if (ret < 0)
+    {
+      return ret;
+    }
+
   /* The truncation has completed without error.  Update the file size */
 
   ff->ff_shared->s_size = length;
